WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:06.080
Was the Lizard a waste of CGI? Does Electro's&nbsp;
makeover make the cut? We put Spider-Man's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:10.560
villains in a head-to-head showdown to answer&nbsp;
one question: Who's the best at being bad?

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:16.560
In "Spider-Man: No Way Home," Willem Dafoe,&nbsp;
Alfred Molina, Thomas Haden Church, Rhys Ifans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:20.320
and Jamie Foxx are all back in their&nbsp;
respective roles as the Green Goblin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:25.280
Doctor Octopus, The Sandman, The Lizard,&nbsp;
and Electro. It's time to line up this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:30.160
quintet of antagonists and rank the maddest,&nbsp;
baddest villains in "Spider-Man: No Way Home."

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:36.160
Scale-obsessed scientist Dr. Curt Connors was&nbsp;
the first foe of Andrew Garfield's Spider-Man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:40.320
back in 2012's "The Amazing Spider-Man."&nbsp;
The Lizard returns in "Spider-Man:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:43.600
No Way Home," but has the least&nbsp;
screen time of all of the villains.

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:48.560
Not even warranting a worthy entrance, his arrival&nbsp;
happens off-screen after he's captured by Doctor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:53.040
Strange while Peter is dealing with Electro&nbsp;
and Sandman. From there, he's left in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:58.400
van for a lengthy portion of the film before&nbsp;
being a forgetful CGI beastie chasing after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:02.880
the web-slinger. Even the method of putting him&nbsp;
out of action is identical to his demise in "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:08.080
Amazing Spider-Man." Sadly, The Lizard feels like&nbsp;
a plus one only there to build out the numbers.

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:12.880
The triple threat that rounds off the original&nbsp;
group of cinematic Spider-Man villains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:18.800
comes in the form of Flint Marko, also known as&nbsp;
The Sandman. Last seen making amends for past&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:23.920
deeds in Tobey Maguire's "Spider-Man 3," Marko is&nbsp;
still a danger to this particular wall-crawler,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:28.400
although not nearly as much as the rest in the&nbsp;
lineup. While prominent for most of the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:32.240
his presence does raise a few questions&nbsp;
in the final act that sees him fighting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:36.400
against our heroes for reasons that aren't&nbsp;
too clear. Instead, he's only put to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:40.480
forefront when it's time for him to bust into&nbsp;
action. While still a feast for the eyes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:44.640
The Sandman makes for an ill-fitting piece&nbsp;
to the much grander puzzle of "No Way Home."

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:50.400
Of all the foes that face off against Spider-Man&nbsp;
in "No Way Home," Electro is without a doubt the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:54.640
one that's had the most drastic change since the&nbsp;
last time we saw him in "The Amazing Spider-Man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:59.840
2." Jamie Foxx's original iteration was a&nbsp;
bumbling scientist with a combover. But the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:04.880
Maxwell Dillon we're reunited with here is, quite&nbsp;
simply, much more like the actor that plays him.

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:08.080
Somehow, Dillon's time in the grid&nbsp;
after his failed effort to become pure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:11.200
energy has turned him into one of&nbsp;
the few criminals in this group&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:15.360
you might want to hang out with. That being&nbsp;
said, his obsession with a new form of energy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:19.840
does make him a force to be reckoned with,&nbsp;
making him an excellent fit for third place.

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:26.320
Spider-Man's enigmatic enemy in director Sam&nbsp;
Raimi's original trilogy, Dr. Otto Octavius is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:30.800
just as charming and equally chilling upon&nbsp;
his return in "Spider-Man: No Way Home."

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:32.320
"Wait, no seriously, what's your actual name?"

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:38.000
And unsurprisingly, Alfred Molina's portrayal of&nbsp;
the scientist we've not seen him play for 17 years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:42.640
is seamless. From his scattered memories of a&nbsp;
fistfight with Spider-Man to battling with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:47.200
voices in his head, Molina makes it crystal&nbsp;
clear why his performance in "Spider-Man 2"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:50.320
made him one of the best comic&nbsp;
book movie villains in history.

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:54.400
We know that Octavius resorted to violence&nbsp;
and criminal means to achieve his goal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:58.800
but we still managed to see the flicker of a&nbsp;
good man in his final moments. In "No Way Home,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:03.200
more time is spent ensuring that the seemingly&nbsp;
lost cause is found and sticks around a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:07.760
longer. Doc Ock feels almost Scrooge-like&nbsp;
here before learning the error of his ways,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:10.160
making his turn back to the light a joy to see.

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:15.840
No one gives their character more attention or&nbsp;
advancement than Willem Dafoe as Norman Osborn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:20.560
also known as the Green Goblin. The first foe&nbsp;
of Tobey Maguire's Spider-Man could've easily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:25.360
phoned in his performance. Instead, he gets back&nbsp;
into both mindsets as the man and the monster&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:30.160
controlling him, making him one of the most&nbsp;
formidable enemies Holland's Spidey has faced.

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:34.880
In "Spider-Man: No Way Home," taking the iconic&nbsp;
but performance-limiting mask out of the equation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:40.000
makes the split between Osborn and Green Goblin&nbsp;
so much more potent and petrifying. Holland's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:45.040
hero is pushed to his limits by a monster on a&nbsp;
glider that effortlessly gets inside his head.

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:49.120
There's no denying that the MCU has&nbsp;
hosted many great villains over the years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:53.680
but Green Goblin reminds us that he helped set&nbsp;
the bar long before the universe even existed.

